ells requires activation of phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase and the GRP1 pleckstrin homology domain</article-title><source>Biochem J</source><year>1998</year><volume>335</volume><fpage>139</fpage><lpage>146</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9742223</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B9"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bi</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Roth</surname><given-names>MG</given-names></name><name><surname>Ktistakis</surname><given-names>NT</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Phosphatidic acid formation by phospholipase D is required for transport from the endoplasmic reticulum to the Golgi complex</article-title><source>Curr Biol</source><year>1997</year><volume>7</volume><fpage>301</fpage><lpage>307</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9133344</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B10"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ktistakis</surname><given-names>NT</given-names></name><name><surname>Brown</surname><given-names>HA</given-names></name><name><surname>Sternweis</surname><given-names>PC</given-names></name><name><surname>Roth</surname><given-names>MG</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Phospholipase D is present on Golgi-enriched membranes and its activation by ADP ribosylation factor is sensitive to brefeldin A</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A</source><year>1995</year><volume>92</volume><fpage>4952</fpage><lpage>4956</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7761430</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B11"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Billah</surname><given-names>MM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Phospholipase D and cell signaling</article-title><source>Curr Opin Immunol</source><year>1993</year><volume>5</volume><fpage>114</fpage><lpage>123</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8383981</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0952-7915(93)90090-F</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Exton</surname><given-names>JH</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Phosphatidylcholine breakdown and signal transduction</article-title><source>Biochim Biophys Acta</source><year>1994</year><volume>1212</volume><fpage>26</fpage><lpage>42</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8155724</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0005-2760(94)90186-4</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Colley</surname><given-names>WC</given-names></name><name><surname>Sung</surname><given-names>TC</given-names></name><name><surname>Roll</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Jenco</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Hammond</surname><given-names>SM</given-names></name><name><surname>Altshuller</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Bar-Sagi</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Morris</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Frohman</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Phospholipase D2, a distinct phospholipase D isoform with novel regulatory properties that provokes cytoskeletal reorganization</article-title><source>Curr Biol</source><year>1997</year><volume>7</volume><fpage>191</fpage><lpage>201</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9395408</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hammond</surname><given-names>SM</given-names></name><name><surname>Altshuller</surname><given-names>YM</given-names></name><name><surname>Sung</surname><given-names>TC</given-names></name><name><surname>Rudge</surname><given-names>SA</given-names></name><name><surname>Rose</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surnam